 desc='A connection between the Dibdibba gravels, Wadi Al-Batin and Kuwait’s water. After 1991, Farouk El-Baz noticed smooth granite and basalt pebbles in Kuwait that are abundant only in western Arabia, and traced on satellite images a dry channel from the Hijaz mountains to Kuwait, partly under dunes, which he called the Kuwait River (El-Baz 1993); he suggested it flowed in wetter times. The hypothesis here is that buried channels of this river beneath Kuwait’s sand sheets still steer groundwater, playas, vegetation, wells and routes.',
 sig='If buried channels can be mapped, they would guide groundwater management, explain the distribution of playas and freshwater lenses, and point archaeological survey to the most likely camp and well locations.',
 ev='Exotic shield-derived gravel (Dibdibba) across northern Kuwait; the Wadi Al-Batin valley; playas along the wadi; freshwater lens at Jahra; historic wells at Al-Atraf and Kadhima near the fan margin.',
 an='In November 1981 the SIR-A radar on Space Shuttle Columbia penetrated dry sand in the eastern Sahara and revealed buried valleys, some nearly as wide as the Nile; Stone Age artefacts occur in alluvium along them, and the buried drainage explains the positions of many playas and oases (McCauley et al. 1982).',
 val='L-band SAR (e.g. ALOS-2, NISAR) processing over north-west Kuwait; mapping of radar-bright buried channels; ground checks with shallow trenches, groundwater levels and salinity; archaeological survey along channel margins.',
 dlist=['Expected radar signature: bright, branching linear features beneath smooth sand sheets.', 'Expected hydrology: fresher, shallower groundwater along channels.', 'Expected archaeology: wells, hearths and lithics clustered on channel margins.'],
